From ce60508d7b2dfb11a6dfdbb9f9c7d0c739fb9c2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VERHILLE Arnaud <gist974@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:30:37 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 04/19] Halt on a double bus fault during reset; take a fault
 on the trace frame

MC68030UM 8.1.1: "if bus error or address error occur during the exception
processing sequence for a reset a double bus fault occurs. The processor
halts". reset() fetched the vectors outside any handler, so a BusError
thrown by the host escaped to the caller instead of halting the CPU. Seen
on a host whose reset vectors are unmapped. reset() now halts on a
BusError or an AddressError.

execute() stacked the trace frame outside any handler too, and a host
BusError escaped the same way. It now goes through processException(),
like a fault during interrupt processing a few lines below: the bus error
exception is taken. MC68030UM 7.5.4: "When a bus error or an address error
occurs during the exception processing sequence for a previous bus error,
a previous address error, or a reset exception, the bus or address error
causes a double bus fault"; a trace exception is not in that list. When
the bus error frame cannot be stacked either, processException() halts:
that is the Quadra 605 whose supervisor stack pointer had been corrupted.

The manuals are not of one voice on this. The M68000 manual supports it
(5.4.4 defines the double bus fault by a bus error during bus error
processing; 6.3.9.1 gives the frame of a bus error taken "during the last
step of exception processing"). MC68020UM 5.5.4 and MC68030UM 7.5.4,
8.1.2 and section 12 give the narrow list; the processing-states chapters
of both say more loosely that a bus error "while in the exception
processing state" is catastrophic. MC68040UM leans the other way: 7.6.3
and the flowcharts of Figures 8-1 and 8-4 send a fault during any
exception processing, interrupts included, to the double bus fault. The
core already takes the bus error on its interrupt path for every model;
this change makes the trace path the same. The 68040 case is open until a
capture.

The status register the bus error frame stacks is the one the trace
exception had already switched to supervisor mode, as on the interrupt
path; what the processor stacks there is not described.
---
 Moira/Moira.cpp | 36 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Moira/Moira.cpp b/Moira/Moira.cpp
index b9ca9bc..55853b4 100644
--- a/Moira/Moira.cpp
+++ b/Moira/Moira.cpp
@@ -157,13 +157,26 @@ Moira::reset()
     fpu.reset();
     completed.armed = false;
 
-    switch (cpuModel) {
+    // A bus or address error while the reset vectors are fetched is a double
+    // bus fault: the processor halts (MC68030UM 8.1.1)
+    try {
 
-        case CPUModel::M68000: reset<Core::C68000>(); break;
-        case CPUModel::M68010: reset<Core::C68010>(); break;
-        
-        default:
-            reset<Core::C68020>();
+        switch (cpuModel) {
+
+            case CPUModel::M68000: reset<Core::C68000>(); break;
+            case CPUModel::M68010: reset<Core::C68010>(); break;
+
+            default:
+                reset<Core::C68020>();
+        }
+
+    } catch (const BusError &) {
+
+        halt();
+
+    } catch (const AddressError &) {
+
+        halt();
     }
 }
 
@@ -214,7 +227,16 @@ Moira::execute()
 
             // Process pending trace exception (if any)
             if (flags & TRACE_EXC) {
-                execException(M68kException::TRACE);
+
+                // A fault while the trace frame is stacked is an ordinary bus
+                // or address error: a double bus fault needs a fault while a
+                // bus error, an address error or a reset is processed
+                // (MC68030UM 7.5.4, 8.1.2)
+                try {
+                    execException(M68kException::TRACE);
+                } catch (const std::exception &exc) {
+                    processException(exc);
+                }
                 goto done;
             }
 
